From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] thermal/drivers/tsens: drop __init from functions reachable
 from probe

tsens_probe() calls priv->ops->init(priv), and every .init member in this driver
points at a function marked __init: init_common() in tsens.c, init_8956() and
init_tsens_v1_no_rpm() in tsens-v1.c, init_tsens_v2_no_rpm() in tsens-v2.c, and
init_8226(), init_8909(), init_8939() and init_9607() in tsens-v0_1.c. Several of
them call init_common(), which is annotated too.

Nothing guarantees that probe runs before the init sections are freed. A deferred
probe, or a platform where the tsens node is probed late, calls into freed text
and oopses. On an IPQ5018 CPE this reproduces reliably: the board Oopses about
9.4 s into boot inside init_tsens_v1_no_rpm, and the only way to get a bootable
system was to disable the tsens node in DT - which leaves the SoC with no thermal
sensing at all.

Drop the annotation from all eight. Fixing only the entry point is not enough
while the init_common() it calls is still annotated. The declaration of
init_common() in tsens.h never carried __init, so it needs no change.

Independently found and fixed with the same mechanism - the same eight functions
across the same four files - by samcday, reported there against a Galaxy A5 SD
where the MODULAR tsens driver resumes deferred probing after its module init
section has been freed:

  https://github.com/samcday/linux/pull/13

That is a different trigger for the same root cause: callbacks living in an init
section that is released while the ops table still points at them. Theirs is a
module's init text with a deferred probe, ours is a built-in's init text with a
late probe on IPQ5018. Two independent reproductions on unrelated hardware, and
neither appears to have been submitted upstream, so this wants sending with both
reported rather than as a single-platform curiosity.

The brace placement on init_8939() and init_8956() is fixed here as well, since
these lines are being touched anyway and checkpatch wants the brace on its own
line.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
